Well, I like the game and have played quite a bit now, but
I think it is too hard too, and agree with whoever said that larn is a lot
more fun.  However, rather than change the game you should change the
documentation.  What bugs me is wasting hour after hour just discovering
which monsters to fight and which to run from.  When I test a wand and
find that it is directional I should get to know right away what the
possibilities are for a directional wand--I should not have to play 300
games just to learn ground rules.   I think the authors might feel the same
way because they have gone to the trouble of giving lots of help ("you
hear a cash register", nice initial inventories, ...).

Nevertheless
the documentation should tell me everything I could discover from a few
hours of eye-balling the source code which maybe is what they assume 
everyone will do anyway.  There should be a complete list of
monsters, spells, wands, items, and such.   And a general description of
the ground rules.  For example I had a level 9 priest who still couldn't
read spells without dipping them in holy water first--and even then he couldn't
cast them.  It is a sort of false advertising to have all these spell books
laying around when they will never be of any use.  Empire does this sort
of thing right by describing the algorithms they use.  Hack should tell
you if a high IQ helps with magic and if a high wisdom does not.

Another grip is this
luck stuff.  Having it is ok, so long as it isn't secret.  A hidden
parameter just makes the game a pain; if they were on the status line
then at least you could learn from your mistakes.


But, I sure am glad someone took the trouble to write it.   And
I hope all these remarks will be regarded as constructive criticism.


